From 846a5297a789f3bf74c44aa98a9d346be0979e53 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "huimin.he" <huimin.he@cybertan.com.tw>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 11:42:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] mtk: mt76: mt7996: keep the regulatory limit with a power
 table

With a power-limits node in DT (or a power table file),
__mt7996_init_txpower() sets chan->max_power to the power of the table
and drops the regulatory limit. hostapd runs it again with the sku_idx
txpower vendor command, so mac80211 then uses the table power as the
BSS txpower and mt76 limits the tables to it instead of the regulatory
power: on RAP736C 5G channel 36 "iw dev" shows 28 dBm (RegDB 23 dBm)
and the 1T1S backoff entries (24 dBm in the table) and the HT20/VHT20
MCS0-2 rates (23.5 dBm) go above 23 dBm.

Limit chan->max_power to the regulatory power in every case, as the
OpenWrt mt76 does (chan->max_power = min(max_reg_power, target_power)).
Before the regulatory domain is applied (max_reg_power 0), keep the
table power.

Signed-off-by: huimin.he <huimin.he@cybertan.com.tw>
---
 mt7996/init.c | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/mt7996/init.c b/mt7996/init.c
index 0d2b234..652c541 100644
--- a/mt7996/init.c
+++ b/mt7996/init.c
@@ -437,7 +437,10 @@ static void __mt7996_init_txpower(struct mt7996_phy *phy,
 
 		target_power += path_delta;
 		target_power = DIV_ROUND_UP(target_power, 2);
-		if (!np && !file)
+		/* keep the regulatory limit with a DT/file power table too,
+		 * as the OpenWrt mt76 does
+		 */
+		if (chan->max_reg_power > 0)
 			chan->max_power = min_t(int, chan->max_reg_power,
 						target_power);
 		else
-- 
